Example:
  egresslock doctor
  egresslock doctor --workloads

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  fail closed (the message names the cause)
  2  usage error
EOF
            ;;
        build-gateway)
            cat <<'EOF'
egresslock build-gateway [--force]

Rebuild the gateway image into this account's Podman image store
from the gateway/ directory next to the engine. No profile config is
loaded. Without --force it skips the build when the image already
matches the base image pinned by the kit; --force rebuilds anyway,
without reusing cached layers.

Arguments and flags:
  --force    rebuild even when the image is already current

Example:
  egresslock build-gateway

Exit codes:
  0  success
  1  fail closed (the message names the cause)
  2  usage error
EOF
            ;;
        teardown)
            cat <<'EOF'
egresslock teardown PROFILE
egresslock teardown all [--yes]
egresslock teardown --orphans

Remove the profile's containers (the anchor and the gateway), its
network, and its firewall chain from the kit's nftables table. When
it was the last profile and no kit network is in use, the shared
table (with the shared p_v6deny chain) is removed too.
A network still in use, an unreadable listing, or a failed removal
keeps the firewall state; the run names why and the next step.

`teardown PROFILE` needs no config: it derives the object names
from the profile name, so shrinking a profile whose config is already
archived still works. A well-formed name with nothing left reports
the objects as not present and exits 0. `v6deny` is reserved for the
shared IPv6-deny chain, so `teardown v6deny` exits 2.

`teardown all` tears down every profile named by a `PROFILE.conf`
file in the account config directory (the file NAMES select; their
contents are never read). It prints the profile names first and, when
more than one profile resolves, requires `--yes` or an interactive
confirmation; a non-interactive run without `--yes` refuses and
removes nothing. Zero config files is a named error pointing at
`--orphans`. A legacy `all.conf` is refused.

`teardown --orphans` needs no config: it removes the kit containers,
networks, and profile chains that no remaining `PROFILE.conf`
owns, then deletes the shared table when no config, kit network, or
kit chain is left. A live profile is never touched; a legacy
`all.conf` or an unreadable config is kept and named. Ownership is
the `egresslock-` name prefix, not who created an object: an
`egresslock-*` container or network you created by hand is treated
as a kit object (an in-use network is kept and named; containers are
force-removed), so rename it first if it must survive. A config
directory that exists but cannot be listed is a named refusal. Use it after
archiving a profile's config, or when uninstalling after
`teardown all --yes`. The retired `--runtime` form exits 2 and names
these two commands.

Every listing is bounded at 5s. If one fails, the run names what it
could not see, prints `incomplete teardown` (or `incomplete sweep`),
and exits 1; a failed removal or a kept network still exits 0 with
its own note. The firewall table is kept when a kit network is kept
in use or the network listing failed (a workload may still be using
one): remove the workload, or re-run once the listing succeeds, to
delete it.

Arguments and flags:
  PROFILE       tear down one profile; its config may already be gone
  all           every profile named by a `PROFILE.conf` file
  --yes         after all only: skip the confirmation
                for a multi-profile run
  --orphans     sweep kit objects no remaining profile config owns

Examples:
  egresslock teardown main
  egresslock teardown all --yes
  egresslock teardown --orphans

Exit codes:
  0  success (including a kept object the run disclosed)
  1  incomplete listing, refused `all` confirmation, or fail closed
  2  usage error
EOF
